WEBVTT

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:02.760
DAVID J. MALAN: Odds are, you
use a computer most every day.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:06.010
But what's going on inside of
that laptop or that desktop.

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:08.676
Well, inside of a computer
are all these 0s and 1s.

00:00:08.676 --> 00:00:10.800
And you may have heard,
indeed, that computers only

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.280
understand 0s and 1s, or the,
so-called, binary system.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.620
But if computers today
can do so much, how can

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.920
they do so much if they can
only speak 0s and 1s and not

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.230
even the entire alphabet
that we humans have.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.000
Well, consider this.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.130
Back in our human world, you might
recognize a pattern of symbols

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:31.590
like this as the number 123.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:32.460
But why is that?

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.670
After all, all I did was draw
three shapes, really, three glyphs,

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.160
or three symbols or
digits on the screen.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.130
But each of these digits
has some predefined meaning.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:44.850
If, in fact, if you went to grade school
like I did and you learned numbers

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.610
in this way, odds are, you might
recall, that this rightmost

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.940
column is the so-called
one's column or one's place.

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.590
Then this is the ten's place.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.510
And then this is the hundred's place.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.349
Now why is that significant?

00:00:56.349 --> 00:01:00.060
Well, the reason that 1, 2,
3-- that pattern represents

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.970
the number we humans know
is 123 is that there's

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.830
a bit of arithmetic going on here.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:10.770
This, after all, means that you
should do 100 times 1 plus 10 times

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.280
2 plus 1 times 3.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:21.930
That, of course, gives us
100 plus 20 plus 3 or 123.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.540
In other words, there's a
pattern to these digits,

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.000
and there's a meaning
behind each of their places.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.930
Well, it turns out that computers
only, indeed, understand 0s and 1s.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.920
They can't count as high as 2 and 3
and 4 in the same way we humans can.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.080
But they use those 0s and
1s in exactly the same way.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.640
In fact, consider this.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:45.292
If we consider this pattern 0,
0, 0, to be inside of a computer,

00:01:45.292 --> 00:01:47.250
and that's what the
computer is thinking, well,

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.610
what number might this be.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:50.360
Well, the spoiler is
that this is actually

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:53.760
going to represent just 0, thankfully,
just like in our human world.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.910
But why is that the case?

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:57.030
And how can we count higher than 0?

00:01:57.030 --> 00:02:00.030
Well, in the computer world,
realize that these places are just

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:00.870
a little different.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.869
This is still the one's
place, but this next one

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:04.440
is not the ten's place anymore.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.239
It's, instead, the two's place.

00:02:06.239 --> 00:02:08.530
And this next place is not
the hundred's place anymore.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:11.412
It's, instead, the four's place.

00:02:11.412 --> 00:02:13.620
And if you think about this,
there's still a pattern.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.370
Previously, it was 1, 10, 100,
and then if we kept going,

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.855
1,000, 10,000 100,000, and so forth.

00:02:20.855 --> 00:02:22.230
And here, too, there's a pattern.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.210
1, 2, 4-- and if I kept going--

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.540
8, 16, and 32.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.930
And so, in our human world,
we tend to use powers of 10.

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:36.810
10 to 0 is 1, 10 to the 1
is 10, 10 to the 2 is 100.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:42.510
And in the computer world, this
is 2 to the 0, which is 1, 2 to 1,

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.780
which is 2, 2 to the 2,
or 2 squared, which is 4.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:48.820
2 to 3 would be 8 and so forth.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:52.050
So all we're doing is changing the
base that we're using, so to speak.

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.930
Instead of powers of 10,
we're using powers of 2.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:03:00.460
And so why is this number 0, 0, 0 the
number we humans know as 0 itself?

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:06.870
Well, again, if we just do the math, 4
times 0 plus 2 times 0 plus 1 times 0

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:13.050
is, of course, 0 plus 0 plus 0 or
the number we humans know as just 0.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.420
Well, what if we do
another number altogether?

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.817
Suppose we do this pattern 0, 0, 1.

00:03:18.817 --> 00:03:20.400
Well, what number does this represent?

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.120
Well, if you consider the same columns--

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.030
1, 2, and 4--

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:29.490
this means we have four times 0, which
is 0, 2 times 0, which is 0, 1 times

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:31.180
1, which is 1.

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.880
So this is the number
we humans know as 1.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:34.380
All right.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.280
So not all that interesting yet,
but what about the number 2?

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:45.190
How, in binary, bi meaning two, and the
2 being a 0 and a 1, how, in binary,

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:47.480
can I now count as high as 2?

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.260
Well, if this is my one's
place, this is my two's place,

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:55.060
and this is my four's place, what digits
do I want to put below those places

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.840
if the only digits I have at my
disposal now are not 0 through 9,

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.940
but just 0 through 1?

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.841
Well, I don't need a 4,
so I can make that a 0.

00:04:03.841 --> 00:04:06.340
I do need 2 if I want to count
to the number we know as two,

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:07.590
so I'm going to put a 1 there.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.390
And I don't need an extra 1
because an extra 1 would give me 3.

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:11.810
So I'm going to do this.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.650
And so a computer, to
store the number we humans

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:19.540
know as two in decimal which
store it in binary as 0, 1, 0.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:23.800
Because that's 4 times 0 plus
2 times 1 plus time 1 times 0,

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.380
which, of course, is going
to give me the number 2.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:27.195
What about 3?

00:04:27.195 --> 00:04:29.050
Well, in the world of
computers, if we again

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.210
have our one's place two's
place and four's place,

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:34.966
how do I now arrange my zeros and ones
to give me the number we know as 3?

00:04:34.966 --> 00:04:36.340
Well, let's see I don't need a 4.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:37.256
That would be too big.

00:04:37.256 --> 00:04:39.340
I do need a 2, and I do need a 1.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.900
And that is now going to give me 3.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:47.110
Because 2 times 1, 1 times 1 is
2, 1 times 1 is 1, 2 plus 1 is 3.

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:48.399
Now what about 4?

00:04:48.399 --> 00:04:49.690
How do I count as high as four?

00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:53.630
Well, it's even easier, perhaps because
we just need a single 1 in this case.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:56.016
We just need 1, 0, 0.

00:04:56.016 --> 00:04:57.890
Because now I have one
1 in the four's place.

00:04:57.890 --> 00:04:58.960
And now let's skip ahead.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:04.450
Suppose I want to count up
as high as, let's say, 7?

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:05.780
What do I do?

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.280
Well, 7, let me take a byte out of that.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.490
So this gives me 4.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:12.000
Let me take a two, that
gives me 4 plus 2, that's 6.
